                   RecipeIngredient(amount: "a pinch", item: "salt"),
                   RecipeIngredient(amount: "4 cups", item: "vegetable oil, for deep frying"),
               ],
               steps: [
                   "Whisk the rice flour, mashed banana, sugar, melted ghee, cardamom, salt, and milk into a thick but pourable batter, like heavy pancake batter that falls in a slow ribbon.",
                   "Rest the batter at room temperature at least an hour; it loosens slightly and picks up the faint tang that marks a proper sel.",
                   "Heat two inches of oil in a wide pot to 350°F, checked with a thermometer or a drop of batter that rises and sizzles at once.",
                   "Load the batter into a squeeze bottle or a zip-top bag with a corner snipped, the honest home stand-in for the traditional pour from the fist.",
                   "Pipe a steady stream into the oil in one continuous circle, about six inches across, joining the ends; nudge it round with a skewer if it drifts.",
                   "Fry until the ring puffs and turns deep gold, about two minutes per side, turning once with two skewers or tongs.",
                   "Drain on a rack, not paper, so the shell stays crisp, and repeat. Serve with yogurt or potato curry, or stack them for the week; they keep for days.",
               ],
               note: "The batter thickness decides everything: too thin and the ring breaks apart in the oil, too thick and the crumb turns heavy instead of springy.",
               shortcut: "Skip the ring and drop spoonfuls like fritters; children will not complain, but it stops being sel roti and becomes very good doughnut holes."),
        Recipe(dishID: "nepal-gundruk", serves: 4, prepMinutes: 10, cookMinutes: 30, difficulty: 1,
               ingredients: [
                   RecipeIngredient(amount: "1 cup", item: "gundruk (Nepali grocer or online; or rinsed sauerkraut plus a handful of chopped kale, a rough but workable stand-in)"),
                   RecipeIngredient(amount: "2", item: "medium potatoes, cut in chunks"),
                   RecipeIngredient(amount: "1/2 cup", item: "cooked soybeans or shelled edamame"),
                   RecipeIngredient(amount: "2", item: "ripe tomatoes, chopped"),
                   RecipeIngredient(amount: "3", item: "dried red chiles"),
                   RecipeIngredient(amount: "4", item: "garlic cloves, sliced"),
                   RecipeIngredient(amount: "1/2 tsp", item: "ground turmeric"),
                   RecipeIngredient(amount: "2 tbsp", item: "mustard oil or vegetable oil"),
                   RecipeIngredient(amount: "1 1/4 tsp", item: "salt"),
                   RecipeIngredient(amount: "4 cups", item: "water"),
               ],
               steps: [
                   "Soak the gundruk in warm water ten minutes to soften, then drain it and chop it roughly; sauerkraut needs only a rinse and a squeeze.",
                   "Heat the oil in a soup pot until shimmering and fry the dried chiles and garlic until the garlic edges turn gold and the chiles darken.",
                   "Add the tomatoes and cook, stirring, until they slump into a rough sauce, three to four minutes.",
                   "Stir in the potatoes and turmeric, then pour in the water and simmer until the potatoes are just tender, about 12 minutes.",
                   "Add the gundruk and soybeans and simmer ten more minutes, so the broth picks up the sour, faintly smoky funk that names the dish.",
                   "Season with salt, taste, and let it sit five minutes; the broth should be thin, sharp, and bracing. Ladle it beside rice or dhindo.",
               ],
               note: "The broth stays thin on purpose; this is a sour winter tonic beside rice, not a stew, and the smell while cooking is the authentic part.",
               shortcut: "The sauerkraut-and-kale version misses the smoky dried-leaf depth but delivers the sour heat of the real jhol on a weeknight."),
        Recipe(dishID: "nepal-dhindo", serves: 4, prepMinutes: 5, cookMinutes: 20, difficulty: 1,
               ingredients: [
                   RecipeIngredient(amount: "1 cup", item: "millet flour (or fine cornmeal as a last resort)"),
                   RecipeIngredient(amount: "1 cup", item: "buckwheat flour"),
                   RecipeIngredient(amount: "4 cups", item: "water"),
                   RecipeIngredient(amount: "1 tbsp", item: "ghee, divided"),
                   RecipeIngredient(amount: "1/2 tsp", item: "salt"),
               ],
               steps: [
                   "Bring the water to a rolling boil in a heavy pot with the salt and half the ghee; the fat keeps the mass from welding itself to the pan.",
                   "Rain in the two flours a handful at a time with one hand while whisking hard with the other, until the mixture thickens past whisking.",
                   "Switch to a strong wooden spoon or paddle and stir vigorously over medium heat, folding from the edges into the center, for eight to ten minutes.",
                   "Keep working it until the dhindo turns glossy, stiff, and elastic and pulls cleanly away from the sides of the pot in one mass.",
                   "Beat in the remaining ghee, cover, and rest two minutes off the heat so it firms enough to hold its shape on a plate.",
                   "Scoop mounds onto plates with a wet spoon and eat by pinching off lumps with your fingers, dipping into gundruk soup or curry, swallowing more than chewing, as the hills do.",
               ],
